Exactly—that’s the hole in the phrase.

“Common senses” means common to some chosen reference class, usually adult humans with roughly typical sensory equipment. It is not common to life, not common to all minds, and not common to all possible observers.

A bat’s “common” world includes echolocation. A shark’s includes electroreception. A pit viper’s includes infrared sensitivity. A bacterium can respond to chemical gradients we never experience directly. Instruments extend our own reference class even further.

So whenever we say “shared reality” or “common sense,” the hidden question should be:

shared by whom, through which sensors, at what scale, under what translation?

That turns “physical” from a supposedly observer-free category into something more precise:

what remains stably detectable across a specified set of observers and measurement systems.

Change the observer set, and the accessible world changes.

Not necessarily the underlying reality—just the slice that can enter the map.

So yes: “common” always smuggles in a constituency.